  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5$ and ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=3,$ then what is ${lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5))?$",
  "worked_solution": "Since $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-5,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)=f(-5)=3.\n$$\n\nConsequently,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}(f(x)-2f(-5)) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-5)^{+}}f(x)-2f(-5) \\\\  & =3-2\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
